WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.970
A ribbon lies along a centimetre ruler.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.300
Its left edge lines up with two, and its right edge

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.490
lines up with nine.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.010
How long is the ribbon?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.520
Look at where it begins and ends, then try to explain

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.550
your first move.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.400
The two marks a position two centimetres from zero.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.900
The nine marks a position nine centimetres from zero.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.970
The question asks for the distance from the first

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.200
position to the second.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.740
Trace that part of the ruler with your finger.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:32.960
Move from two to three.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.420
That is the first centimetre.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.720
From three to four is the second.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.360
Every small step adds one centimetre.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.500
Keep going until you reach nine.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.010
There are seven gaps of one centimetre, so the ribbon is

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.460
seven centimetres long.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.850
The nine centimetres from zero to the end includes two

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.720
centimetres before the ribbon begins.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.700
Remove those two centimetres.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.400
Nine minus two leaves seven.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.130
You can count eight marked points across the ribbon

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.040
because both ends have a mark.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.420
Each centimetre lives in the space between two marks.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.800
Slide the ribbon two centimetres to the left.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.320
Watch both ends move the same distance.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.000
It starts at zero and reaches seven.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.050
The two endpoints changed together, and the span

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.540
stayed seven centimetres.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.140
That is another way to check our answer.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.780
The same idea works with time.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.350
A film starts at seven fifteen in the evening and ends

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.320
at nine oh five.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:29.940
How many minutes pass?

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.200
Draw a timeline.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.600
Put seven fifteen on the left.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.650
Mark eight o'clock and nine o'clock along the way,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.260
and stop at nine oh five.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.100
From seven fifteen to eight o'clock

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.510
is forty-five minutes.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.810
The next hour gives sixty minutes.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.510
From nine o'clock to the end is five more.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.510
Add forty-five, sixty and five.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.090
The total is one hundred and ten minutes.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.330
That is one hour and fifty minutes.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.830
Label the two positions.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.130
Find the length, or the time, between them.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.630
Use equal steps and check the units.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.590
Now try your own timeline.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.200
A camera starts filming at ten forty-seven in the

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.890
morning and stops at twelve oh six that day.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.650
How many minutes does it record?

